2186045417] [17592186105964 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105964 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186105964 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d489d7ad-a521-5b24-bbbd-5997d33ba8e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106925 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106925 #edb/ref 1061 464354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be7df47b-44a5-5a3f-b532-2fc81385da20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106926 #edb/ref 1061 464355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d16dd58-a46a-51e5-8c79-4e7cf4e9b6c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106927 #edb/ref 1061 464358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04e9769b-9cba-55d2-a3b5-ab09464bb0b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106928 #edb/ref 1061 464359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58a570ee-c7cc-5be3-bfd4-c8e0901c10f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106929 #edb/ref 1061 464362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f33f722f-f69e-5a75-bfb3-339e2cb1e5bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106930 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106930 #edb/ref 1061 464363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af06265a-9f99-50db-9f20-6b0f473380d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106931 #edb/ref 1061 464366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1974a4e-dd77-5808-a604-05fb1fa36604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107078 #edb/ref 1064 "Geometrically, the absolute value of the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ represents the volume enclosed by the parallelepiped whose sides are formed by the three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the parallelepiped shown below. The sides of this parallelepiped are formed by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelepiped is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV = |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107078 #edb/ref 1065 2956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107078 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5917536-1cb1-5fba-9f81-7d866e832dcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107078 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107079 #edb/ref 1064 "Three vectors are said to be **coplanar** if they are parallel to the same plane.\n\nWhen we have three coplanar vectors, the parallelepiped they span is *flat* on the plane, and therefore its volume is zero. Consequently, the triple product of the vectors is zero as well.\n\nSo, if three vectors are coplanar, then their triple product is zero. The converse is also true: if the triple product of three nonzero vectors is zero, then the vectors are coplanar.\n\nTherefore, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar $\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad$    $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107079 #edb/ref 1065 2957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107079 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3012c88-77aa-5271-ad5f-b2176f99ba15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107079 #edb/ref 1067 "The Volume Spanned by Coplanar Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107084 #edb/ref 1064 "We have been using the fact that the volume of a parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV =  |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nBut why is this formula true? To understand why, remember that the volume of a parallelepiped can be obtained by multiplying the area of the base by the height.\n\nAs we can see in the diagram below, \n\n- the area of the base is given by $A = | \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} |,$ and\n\n- the height is given by $h = |\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}}\\:\\mathbf{a}|.$\n\n![](images/cf/cfc492127bed6d6929518a708288691c5fe79e1842be6ecf92666bed6b03200b.png)\n\nTo compute the volume, we will multiply the area of the base by the height. First, though, let's write down the expression for the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =|{\\text{proj}}_{b\\times c}\\,a| \\\\  & =|\\frac{a\\cdot (b\\times c)}{|b\\times c|}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying the area of the base by the height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =A\\cdot h \\\\  & =|b\\times c|\\cdot \\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|} \\\\  & =|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107084 #edb/ref 1065 3309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107084 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6b371cde-1beb-5aa2-b564-0bae79e2faf1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107084 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof That the Volume of a Parallelepiped is Given by the Absolute Value of the Triple Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107085 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we are not given the vectors that represent the sides of a parallelepiped. Instead, we might be given the points at which the vertices lie.\n\nTo compute the volume of a parallelepiped given the vertices, we can still use a similar process as before. The only difference is that we have to start by computing the displacement vectors between the points.\n\nOnce we have the displacement vectors, then we can find the desired volume by computing the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nLet's see an example of this." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107085 #edb/ref 1065 3310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107085 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e078c251-f4ae-5e91-abd6-3272b43e7b22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107085 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Given the Coordinates of the Vertices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260983 #edb/ref 1080 "14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f850623-1cf0-5b7f-b020-c07acfebbd8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261224 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261224 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261224 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9baefb07-c1d1-5b54-860e-b68104387cb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261451 #edb/ref 1080 "42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261451 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261451 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b255695f-5469-5e72-bef9-0b28e1845792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261886 #edb/ref 1080 "28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261886 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261886 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4a3d805-1618-5717-aa44-a03a5852416b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261888 #edb/ref 1080 "56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261888 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261888 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4d4c9d9-60a8-5e94-b5ef-2dd4a95c4889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186261886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262239 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "40927668-f3a3-5ce4-80d8-9ec8828fb9da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262892 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262892 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $p=⟨2,2,3⟩,$ $q=⟨4,2,-1⟩,$ and $r=⟨-5,0,3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59779a24-408f-5832-999c-31c9f5625f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262892 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))-2(4\\cdot 3-(-5)\\cdot (-1))+3(4\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12-14+30 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|28|=28.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293019 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "153692ac-17dc-5340-8ab8-c764df7ca660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294015 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294015 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294015 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294015 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294015 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1b34c4d0-3f40-5fab-a961-3f1c1324f59d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295169 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295169 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295169 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295169 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,3,0⟩$, $b=⟨0,4,2⟩$, and $c=⟨2,k,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61f3e6a0-31a0-537c-a9de-e6c7ae59826e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295169 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\na\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ k & 5\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot k)-3(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2)=(20-2k)+12 \\\\  & =32-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 32-2k & =0 \\\\ -2k & =-32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨{k}^{2},-2,5⟩$, $y=⟨1,3,4⟩$, and $z=⟨0,-1,2⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n**In ascending order:** $\\,$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}\n$\\,$\nand\n$\\,$\n$k=${{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe6d904a-8846-5cf7-a75e-99f480622386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379355 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-1))+2(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0)+5(1\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(6+4)+2(2)+5(-1) \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}+4-5 \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}$ or $k=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379356 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379356 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186458954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379356 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379356 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379356 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186458954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379356 #edb